NETWORKING: how Genera talks to the Linux host

The README says what to run. This file says what those pieces are, why
they are needed, and what to check when something does not work.


1. THE PICTURE

Genera believes it is a computer of its own, attached to a network,
with a second computer beside it that holds its files. Under this
emulator both "computers" are your one Linux machine:

      Genera (inside the genera process)        Linux host
      address 10.0.0.2                           address 10.0.0.1
             |                                        |
             +---------------- tap0 ------------------+
                    a private, software-only network

      over that network Genera asks the host for
          - the time            (once, at boot)
          - files, by NFS       (sys.sct: sources, fonts, documentation)

Genera calls itself DIS-LOCAL-HOST and the Linux machine DIS-EMB-HOST.
Those names are built into the distribution world.

The addresses above are the ones in dot.VLM and are the defaults of
every script here. If you change them, change them everywhere: the
genera.network line in .VLM, setup-tap.sh, and nfs2d.py --bind.


2. THE TAP DEVICE

tap0 is a virtual network card: Linux treats it as an Ethernet
interface, but instead of hardware the other end is a program, here
genera. Packets Genera sends appear on tap0; packets Linux sends to
10.0.0.2 through tap0 are handed to Genera.

The network line in .VLM ties the two ends together:

     genera.network: tap0:INTERNET|10.0.0.2;mask=255.255.255.0;gateway=10.0.0.1

     tap0         the device genera opens
     10.0.0.2     Genera's own address
     10.0.0.1     the host's address, which tap0 must carry

Rules:

     - The device must exist before genera starts. Without it genera
       exits with "TUNSETIFF: Operation not permitted".
     - It must be owned by the user who runs genera; setup-tap.sh sees
       to that, so genera itself never needs root.
     - Host and Genera must be on the same subnet.
     - The device is not permanent. It is gone after a reboot.

Create it with

     sudo ./setup-tap.sh

and look at it with

     ip addr show tap0


3. THE NFS SERVER

Genera keeps almost nothing on a disk of its own. Its system sources,
fonts and all of the on-line documentation live in the sys.sct
directory tree, which it reads from the host with NFS.

Genera speaks only NFS version 2, over UDP. Current Linux kernels no
longer serve version 2 at all, so the ordinary NFS server (nfsd,
/etc/exports) cannot be used. nfs2d.py is a small stand-alone server
for exactly this job. It provides, on the host address only:

     UDP/TCP 111     portmapper    (how Genera finds the other two)
     UDP/TCP 2049    mount and NFS version 2
     UDP/TCP 37      the time protocol (section 4)

It is meant for Genera on the tap device and nothing else. It does no
authentication, so do not bind it to an address other machines can
reach.

What it serves. --root names a directory that Genera sees as "/". The
distribution world looks for /cdrom/sys.sct, so the directory must
contain a "cdrom" entry (a symbolic link is fine) leading to the
directory that holds sys.sct:

     /srv/genera/cdrom  ->  /path/to/opengenera-cd
     /path/to/opengenera-cd/sys.sct/...

Starting it by hand:

     sudo ./nfs2d.py --root /srv/genera

Root is needed only to open ports below 1024; the server then switches
to your own user and reads files as you. Add -v to log every request.

Read-only or writable. By default Genera cannot change anything. With
--rw it can create, write and delete files below --root, as your user.
You need that only if you want to save your own files from Genera onto
the host. Serve a separate directory for that purpose rather than
making your sys.sct copy writable.

Restarting nfs2d.py while Genera is running invalidates the file
handles Genera holds. Restart Genera as well.

Telling Genera where the files are. These two forms, typed at the
"Command:" prompt, have to be given after every boot of the
distribution world:

     (si:login-to-sys-host)
     (fs:set-logical-pathname-host "SYS"
       :translations '(("**;*.*" "dis-emb-host:/cdrom/sys.sct/**/*.*")))

The lasting cure is to define a site and save a world of your own; see
docs/Open_Genera_Installation_Guide.pdf and docs/Site_Operations.pdf.


4. THE TIME

At boot Genera broadcasts a request for the time on its network (UDP
port 37, the "time protocol" of RFC 868). nfs2d.py answers it.

If nothing answers, Genera asks you to type the date and time in the
cold load window. Do not answer: the emulator cannot set a hardware
clock, so Genera then stops with "Unable to set calendar clock" and
its network stays down. Fix the cause and start genera again.

Genera assumes the US Eastern time zone until a site is defined, so
its clock may be off by whole hours from yours.


5. FIREWALLS

Traffic from Genera arrives at the host on tap0 like traffic from any
other machine, and a host firewall that drops incoming traffic drops
it too. Pings usually still get through, which makes this easy to
overlook. Allow the Genera subnet on the tap device only. With ufw:

     sudo ufw allow in on tap0 from 10.0.0.0/24

With firewalld, put tap0 in the trusted zone:

     sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=trusted --add-interface=tap0
     sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Unlike the tap device, a firewall rule survives a reboot.


6. WHAT RESETS, AND WHEN

     after a reboot of the host      tap0 is gone
                                     nfs2d.py is not running
     after every start of genera     the two forms of section 3

./run-genera.sh takes care of the first two when it finds them
missing. The forms must be typed by hand.


7. LIMITS

     - Genera can reach the host and nothing else. It has no route to
       your real network or to the Internet. That would need IP
       forwarding and address translation on the host, and a matching
       site definition in Genera; neither is set up here.
     - The distribution world offers no network services of its own
       ("Servers are currently disabled"), so you cannot log in to
       Genera from the host.
     - nfs2d.py implements NFS version 2 only, which limits files to
       2 gigabytes. No version 3 or 4 client can use it.


8. WHEN IT DOES NOT WORK

Check in this order; each step depends on the one before.

  genera exits at once with "TUNSETIFF: Operation not permitted"
       The tap device is missing or belongs to another user.
       sudo ./setup-tap.sh

  Is Genera on the network?
       ping 10.0.0.2
       It answers from about ten seconds after genera starts. If not,
       compare the addresses in .VLM with "ip addr show tap0".

  Is the server running and listening?
       ss -lun | grep 10.0.0.1
       should list ports 37, 111 and 2049. If it was started by
       run-genera.sh:  systemctl status genera-nfs2d

  Did Genera's requests arrive?
       journalctl -u genera-nfs2d -o cat | grep 10.0.0.2
       A "TIME" line appears at every boot of Genera and an "MNT" line
       when it first reaches for a file. Ping works but neither line
       appears: a firewall is dropping the traffic (section 5). To see
       the packets themselves:  sudo tcpdump -ni tap0

  Genera asks for the date and time
       Its time request was not answered. Server not running, or a
       firewall. See section 4; restart genera after fixing it.

  The Document Examiner lists the books but shows no text, or Genera
  reports that a file or host cannot be found
       The two forms of section 3 were not typed in this session, or
       --root has no cdrom/sys.sct below it. Check from the host:
       ls /srv/genera/cdrom/sys.sct

  "Error: VALUE-ERROR ... [SetModifierMapping]" in the cold load window
       An old genera binary. The fix is in life-support/console.c;
       rebuild.
